-- title:
--   The chart ring A_R(S) has fraction field F
-- statement:
--   Let $R$ be a commutative domain, let $K_0$ be a field equipped with an $R$-algebra structure making it a fraction ring of $R$, and let $F$ be a field which is an algebra over both $R$ and $K_0$, the two structures being compatible in the sense that the tower $R \to K_0 \to F$ commutes. Let $S \subseteq F$ be a subset, and assume that $F$ is algebraic over the intermediate field $K_0(S) =$ `IntermediateField.adjoin K₀ S` generated by $S$ over $K_0$ inside $F$. Write $A_R(S)$ for `chartAlg R F S`, the $R$-subalgebra of $F$ whose elements are exactly those $x \in F$ that are integral over the $R$-subalgebra $R[S] =$ `Algebra.adjoin R S` of $F$. The conclusion is that $F$ is a fraction ring of $A_R(S)$ for the inclusion $A_R(S) \subseteq F$: the structure map sends the non-zero-divisors of $A_R(S)$ to units of $F$, every element of $F$ is a quotient of images of elements of $A_R(S)$, and the map is injective in the appropriate sense.
--
--   This is the statement that the chart rings occurring in the two-chart integral model of a curve — the integral closures of $R[S]$ in $F$ — have $F$ itself as fraction field; classically, the integral closure of a domain in an algebraic extension of its fraction field has that extension as its fraction field. It is used throughout the analysis of the two-chart model, for instance in the comparison of chart rings with tensor products over integrally closed bases and in the construction of étale coordinates and localisations at chart functions.
